// FiqhHub — Gender Interaction (Ahkam al-Ikhtilat) Fatwas
window.FATWAS_INTERACTION = [
{id:'it001',cat:'interaction',sub:'Looking',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the rules about looking at non-mahram persons?',a:'Men must lower their gaze from non-mahram women — looking with desire or lust is haram. A casual glance that is not with desire and is unintentional is not sinful. Looking at the face and hands of a non-mahram woman is permissible without lust according to many scholars. Women must similarly lower their gaze from non-mahram men. Looking for the purpose of marriage consideration is permissible — one may look at the face, hair, and general appearance of a potential spouse.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['looking','gaze','non-mahram','desire','hijab']},
{id:'it002',cat:'interaction',sub:'Looking',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the rules about looking at non-mahram persons?',a:'Lowering the gaze (ghadd al-basar) is Quranic (24:30-31). Deliberately looking at a non-mahram with desire is haram. Looking at the permissible parts (face and hands) without desire for a legitimate purpose (identification, business) is fine. Looking at a potential spouse is explicitly permitted by hadith to facilitate good marriage decisions.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['looking','gaze','non-mahram','desire','hijab']},
{id:'it003',cat:'interaction',sub:'Looking',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the rules about looking at non-mahram persons?',a:'The Quran commands both men and women to lower their gaze. This doesn\'t mean never looking — it means avoiding lustful or prolonged gazing. Looking at non-mahram for medical treatment, education, business, and other necessities is permissible. The key criterion is presence or absence of shahwah (desire). If looking triggers desire, it becomes haram regardless of what is being looked at.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['looking','gaze','non-mahram','desire','hijab']},
{id:'it004',cat:'interaction',sub:'Handshaking',marja:'sistani',q:'Is handshaking between non-mahram men and women permissible?',a:'Physical contact (including handshaking) between non-mahram men and women is not permissible on the basis of obligatory precaution. This applies when there is skin-to-skin contact. Handshaking while wearing gloves or with a barrier is technically different but still best avoided. In professional settings in non-Muslim countries, one may explain their religious practice politely.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['handshaking','contact','non-mahram','physical','touching']},
{id:'it005',cat:'interaction',sub:'Handshaking',marja:'khamenei',q:'Is handshaking between non-mahram men and women permissible?',a:'Handshaking with non-mahram is haram. Alternative greetings (placing hand on heart, nodding respectfully) are sufficient and increasingly understood internationally. If one is in an extremely uncomfortable position where refusal would cause significant harm (not mere awkwardness), some scholars allow flexibility, but this should not become habitual.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['handshaking','contact','non-mahram','physical','touching']},
{id:'it006',cat:'interaction',sub:'Handshaking',marja:'makarem',q:'Is handshaking between non-mahram men and women permissible?',a:'Direct skin contact between non-mahram is prohibited. This is based on clear hadith. In modern professional contexts, one can politely decline and offer an alternative greeting. Most people understand and respect religious practices when explained kindly. Using a barrier (glove) is a matter of scholarly debate but best avoided.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['handshaking','contact','non-mahram','physical','touching']},
{id:'it007',cat:'interaction',sub:'Workplace',marja:'sistani',q:'Can men and women work together in mixed environments?',a:'Working in mixed environments is permissible provided Islamic guidelines are observed: (1) Proper hijab for women, (2) Professional conduct without khalwah (seclusion of one man and one woman), (3) Lowering the gaze, (4) Avoiding unnecessary casual interaction, (5) Maintaining professional boundaries. Islam does not prohibit women from working or earning — Sayyida Khadijah (sa) was a successful businesswoman. The conditions apply equally to both genders.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['workplace','mixed','gender','hijab','professional']},
{id:'it008',cat:'interaction',sub:'Workplace',marja:'khamenei',q:'Can men and women work together in mixed environments?',a:'Mixed workplaces are a reality of modern life and are permissible with adherence to Islamic ethics: proper dress code, professional behavior, avoiding fitnah (temptation). Women have the right to work, education, and public participation. The Islamic framework doesn\'t isolate genders but establishes ethical boundaries for interaction. The workplace should maintain respectful professional culture.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['workplace','mixed','gender','hijab','professional']},
{id:'it009',cat:'interaction',sub:'Workplace',marja:'makarem',q:'Can men and women work together in mixed environments?',a:'Co-education and mixed workplaces are permissible with conditions. Islam encourages women\'s participation in society — women were active in early Islamic communities in education, commerce, healthcare, and even battlefield support. The modern workplace should adapt Islamic principles: professional conduct, appropriate dress, respectful interaction, and avoidance of situations that compromise modesty.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['workplace','mixed','gender','hijab','professional']},
{id:'it010',cat:'interaction',sub:'Online Interaction',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the rulings on online communication between non-mahram?',a:'Online communication follows the same principles as in-person interaction: (1) Content must be appropriate and professional, (2) Private messaging that leads to emotional attachment or inappropriate content is haram, (3) Video calls where hijab is observed are treated like in-person meetings, (4) Social media use should maintain Islamic ethics. The medium doesn\'t change the ruling — what is haram in person is haram online.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['online','digital','communication','social media','non-mahram']},
{id:'it011',cat:'interaction',sub:'Online Interaction',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the rulings on online communication between non-mahram?',a:'Digital interaction is subject to the same fiqhi rulings as physical interaction. Chatting that leads to haram relationships is forbidden. Social media profiles should observe Islamic guidelines. Sending personal photos to non-mahram is problematic. Online platforms for legitimate purposes (education, business, professional networking) are permissible with proper conduct.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['online','digital','communication','social media','non-mahram']},
{id:'it012',cat:'interaction',sub:'Online Interaction',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the rulings on online communication between non-mahram?',a:'The internet has created new challenges for gender interaction. Guidelines: maintain the same standards online as offline, avoid private prolonged conversations with non-mahram without purpose, be cautious about emotional entanglement through messaging, use video features only when necessary and with proper hijab. Parents should guide young users in Islamic digital ethics.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['online','digital','communication','social media','non-mahram']},

{id:'it013',cat:'interaction',sub:'Hijab',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the requirements of hijab for women?',a:'The obligatory hijab requires covering the entire body except the face (from forehead to chin, ear to ear) and the hands (to the wrists). Hair must be completely covered. The clothing must not be tight-fitting such that it outlines the body shape, must not be transparent, and should not be excessively attention-drawing. Feet covering is obligatory on the basis of obligatory precaution. Makeup on the face in public where it attracts attention from non-mahram men is problematic.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['hijab','covering','women','requirements','modesty']},
{id:'it014',cat:'interaction',sub:'Hijab',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the requirements of hijab for women?',a:'Hijab requirements: covering all of the body except the oval of the face and hands to the wrist. The covering must be: non-transparent, not tight or body-revealing, covering hair completely, and dignified. The specific style (chador, scarf, etc.) is not mandated — the conditions matter, not the particular garment. The purpose is modesty (haya), not cultural conformity. What constitutes \'attention-drawing\' varies by context and culture.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['hijab','covering','women','requirements','modesty']},
{id:'it015',cat:'interaction',sub:'Hijab',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the requirements of hijab for women?',a:'Hijab covers the body except the face and hands. The clothing should be: loose enough to not reveal body shape, opaque, and not ostentatious. There is no specific mandated style — any clothing meeting these conditions is valid. The Quran says to "draw their veils over their chests" (24:31) and "lower their outer garments" (33:59). The spirit is dignified modesty, protection of women\'s agency, and redirecting social interaction toward intellect rather than appearance.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['hijab','covering','women','requirements','modesty']},
{id:'it016',cat:'interaction',sub:'Mahram',marja:'sistani',q:'Who are considered mahram (unmarriageable kin)?',a:'Mahram relationships where marriage is permanently forbidden include: (1) Blood relatives: parents, children, siblings, grandparents, grandchildren, uncles, aunts, nephews, nieces, (2) Marriage-related: mother-in-law, father-in-law, stepchildren (after consummation), son/daughter-in-law, (3) Breastfeeding (ridha\'ah): with specific conditions, a wet nurse and the nursed child develop mahram relationships equivalent to blood ties. A woman need not observe hijab before her mahram men, and physical contact (handshake, etc.) is permissible.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['mahram','relatives','marriage','blood','unmarriageable']},
{id:'it017',cat:'interaction',sub:'Mahram',marja:'khamenei',q:'Who are considered mahram (unmarriageable kin)?',a:'Mahram categories: nasab (blood) — parents, grandparents, children, grandchildren, siblings, uncles, aunts, nephews, nieces; sabab (marriage) — spouse\'s parents, spouse\'s children from previous marriage (after consummation), children\'s spouses; ridha\'ah (breastfeeding) — equivalent to blood relations if conditions met (specific number of feedings, within 2 years, from one husband\'s milk). Cousin marriage is permissible in Islam as cousins are NOT mahram.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['mahram','relatives','marriage','blood','unmarriageable']},
{id:'it018',cat:'interaction',sub:'Marriage Proposal',marja:'sistani',q:'Can a man look at a woman he intends to marry?',a:'Yes, Islam specifically permits looking at a potential spouse for marriage consideration. A man may look at the woman\'s face, hands, and general appearance (some scholars extend this to hair and figure while clothed). This should be done respectfully, not repeatedly, and only when there is genuine marriage intent. The woman should be in her natural state (not excessively made up). Similarly, the woman has the right to see the man. This is to facilitate informed marriage decisions.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['marriage','looking','proposal','permission','spouse']},
{id:'it019',cat:'interaction',sub:'Marriage Proposal',marja:'khamenei',q:'Can a man look at a woman he intends to marry?',a:'Looking at a potential spouse is explicitly permitted by hadith. The Prophet (s) told a companion who was considering marriage to "go and look at her." The permission covers: face, hands, and general appearance. Some scholars allow seeing the hair and figure. The key conditions: genuine intent to marry, not for entertainment, respectful manner, and limited occasions. The woman\'s consent to being seen should be obtained.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['marriage','looking','proposal','permission','spouse']},
{id:'it020',cat:'interaction',sub:'Marriage Proposal',marja:'makarem',q:'Can a man look at a woman he intends to marry?',a:'Islam encourages seeing one\'s potential spouse before marriage. The hadith clearly establish this permission. Both parties have the right to see each other. The purpose is preventing later disappointment and ensuring physical compatibility. Meetings should be in appropriate settings — many scholars recommend meeting in the presence of family members. Photographs may also serve this purpose in modern contexts.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['marriage','looking','proposal','permission','spouse']},
{id:'it021',cat:'interaction',sub:'Hijab',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the requirements of hijab for women?',a:'Women must cover their entire body from non-mahram men except the face (from forehead to chin) and hands (up to wrists). The covering must: (1) Not be so tight as to show body shape, (2) Not be transparent, (3) Not be attention-grabbing (tabarruj). The feet are debated — covering them is recommended/obligatory precaution. Hair must be completely covered. The color and style of hijab are not specified in fiqh — modesty is the principle, not a specific garment.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['hijab','women','covering','modesty','requirements']},
{id:'it022',cat:'interaction',sub:'Hijab',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the requirements of hijab for women?',a:'Hijab requirements: covering all body except face and hands from non-mahram, with loose-fitting, non-transparent clothing. The feet should be covered on obligatory precaution. Makeup that attracts attention is problematic. The chador is the best form of hijab but is not the only valid one. Any clothing that meets the modesty requirements is acceptable.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['hijab','women','covering','modesty','requirements']},
{id:'it023',cat:'interaction',sub:'Hijab',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the requirements of hijab for women?',a:'The hijab should cover everything except the face and hands. The clothing should be: loose, opaque, not perfumed in public, and not resembling typical male clothing. Head covering must fully contain all hair. Islam specifies principles of modesty, not specific garments — this is why hijab styles vary across cultures while all meeting the core requirements.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['hijab','women','covering','modesty','requirements']},
{id:'it024',cat:'interaction',sub:'Hijab',marja:'sistani',q:'Is the face veil (niqab) obligatory?',a:'The niqab (face veil) is not obligatory in Shia jurisprudence. The face and hands are exempt from covering requirements. Wearing niqab is a personal choice and is considered a form of additional modesty. Some women choose to wear it as a precautionary measure or out of personal piety, which is commendable but not required.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['niqab','face veil','obligation','hijab','choice']},
{id:'it025',cat:'interaction',sub:'Hijab',marja:'khamenei',q:'Is the face veil (niqab) obligatory?',a:'Niqab is not wajib. The face may be shown as long as it is not adorned with conspicuous makeup that would attract attention. Covering the face is mustahab (recommended) but not obligatory. Women should not be pressured to wear niqab nor criticized for choosing to wear it — both positions are valid.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['niqab','face veil','obligation','hijab','choice']},
{id:'it026',cat:'interaction',sub:'Hijab',marja:'makarem',q:'Is the face veil (niqab) obligatory?',a:'Face covering is not among the obligations of hijab. The Quranic verse mentions "what is apparent" as excepted from covering, and the predominant Shia view is that this includes the face and hands. However, in situations where showing the face might cause fitnah, additional covering becomes precautionary.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['niqab','face veil','obligation','hijab','choice']},
{id:'it027',cat:'interaction',sub:'Mahram',marja:'sistani',q:'Who are considered mahram relatives?',a:'Mahram relatives (those with whom interaction is free from hijab requirements) include: By blood: parents, grandparents, children, grandchildren, siblings, uncles, aunts, nieces, nephews. By marriage: father-in-law, mother-in-law, son-in-law, daughter-in-law, stepparents, stepchildren. By breastfeeding: those who share a milk relationship (specific conditions apply — at least 15 consecutive feedings from one woman). Cousins are NOT mahram.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['mahram','relatives','family','hijab','blood']},
{id:'it028',cat:'interaction',sub:'Mahram',marja:'khamenei',q:'Who are considered mahram relatives?',a:'Mahram categories: (1) Nasabi (blood): seven categories listed in Quran 4:23, (2) Sababi (marriage): in-laws as specified, (3) Ridha\'i (breastfeeding): under specific conditions of quantity and timing. The rules are clearly defined — when in doubt, treat the relationship as non-mahram. Adopted children do not automatically become mahram.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['mahram','relatives','family','hijab','blood']},
{id:'it029',cat:'interaction',sub:'Mahram',marja:'makarem',q:'Who are considered mahram relatives?',a:'Mahram relationships are precisely defined in Quran and Sunnah. A common mistake: cousins are NOT mahram despite close family relationships. Another important point: a woman\'s brother-in-law (husband\'s brother) is NOT her mahram — the famous hadith says "the brother-in-law is death" meaning extra caution is needed. Mahram relationships through breastfeeding require specific conditions.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['mahram','relatives','family','hijab','blood']},
{id:'it030',cat:'interaction',sub:'Mixed Gatherings',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the rules for attending mixed social gatherings?',a:'Mixed social gatherings are permissible with conditions: (1) Proper hijab observed, (2) No haram activities (alcohol, inappropriate music), (3) Maintaining modesty in behavior and conversation, (4) Avoiding intimate one-on-one situations with non-mahram. Wedding celebrations, professional events, and community gatherings where these conditions are met are all permissible. Islam encourages social interaction within ethical boundaries.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['mixed','gatherings','social','events','permissible']},
{id:'it031',cat:'interaction',sub:'Mixed Gatherings',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the rules for attending mixed social gatherings?',a:'Attending mixed gatherings is permissible when Islamic standards are maintained. Complete segregation of genders in all social situations is not required by Islam — the early Muslim community had mixed interactions in mosques, markets, and social events. The key is maintaining proper conduct, dress, and avoiding situations that lead to sin.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['mixed','gatherings','social','events','permissible']},
{id:'it032',cat:'interaction',sub:'Mixed Gatherings',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the rules for attending mixed social gatherings?',a:'Social interaction is natural and needed. Mixed gatherings for legitimate purposes (education, work, community, celebrations) are fine with proper conduct. Islam promotes social engagement, not isolation. However, gatherings that feature haram elements (alcohol, inappropriate entertainment, excessive free mixing) should be avoided. The atmosphere should be one where faith feels comfortable.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['mixed','gatherings','social','events','permissible']},
{id:'it033',cat:'interaction',sub:'Marriage Proposals',marja:'sistani',q:'How should the process of seeking a spouse work islamically?',a:'The Islamic process: (1) Identify a potential spouse through family, friends, community, or even appropriate online platforms, (2) Investigate their character, faith, and compatibility through trusted sources, (3) Arrange a meeting with proper setting (family presence recommended), (4) Look at the potential spouse (face, hair, build) — this is explicitly permitted, (5) Ask questions about important matters (religious practice, life goals, expectations), (6) Perform istikharah if undecided, (7) Proceed to engagement and marriage contract.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['marriage','proposal','spouse','seeking','compatibility']},
{id:'it034',cat:'interaction',sub:'Marriage Proposals',marja:'makarem',q:'How should the process of seeking a spouse work islamically?',a:'Seeking a spouse is encouraged and should not be stigmatized. The process should be: practical (investigating compatibility), respectful (maintaining dignity), transparent (honest about expectations), and faith-centered (prioritizing religious commitment). The Prophet (s) said: "When someone whose religion and character please you proposes, accept them." Modern methods of meeting (apps, social media) are permissible if conducted within Islamic guidelines.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['marriage','proposal','spouse','seeking','compatibility']},
{id:'it035',cat:'interaction',sub:'Marriage Proposals',marja:'khamenei',q:'How should the process of seeking a spouse work islamically?',a:'Marriage is half of faith — seeking a spouse is a virtuous act. The process should involve: research into the person\'s background and character, supervised meeting, honest discussion of expectations, and consulting with trusted elders. Both parties have the right to accept or reject. Pressure to accept or reject based on wealth, status, or appearance alone goes against prophetic guidance.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['marriage','proposal','spouse','seeking','compatibility']},
{id:'it036',cat:'interaction',sub:'Khalwah',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on khalwah (being alone with non-mahram)?',a:'Being alone in a closed private space with a non-mahram of the opposite gender is strongly discouraged and considered haram by many scholars if there is risk of sin. This is called khalwah. Public places (restaurants, offices with glass walls) where others can see do not constitute khalwah. The Prophet (s) warned against seclusion with non-mahram. Practical measures: keep doors open, meet in public spaces, have a third person present.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['khalwah','seclusion','alone','non-mahram','private']},
{id:'it037',cat:'interaction',sub:'Khalwah',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the ruling on khalwah (being alone with non-mahram)?',a:'Khalwah with non-mahram is to be avoided. In professional settings, meetings should occur in visible spaces. In medical contexts, having a chaperone is recommended when possible. Video calls may be an alternative to in-person meetings when khalwah would otherwise occur. The principle is preventing situations that could lead to temptation or accusations.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['khalwah','seclusion','alone','non-mahram','private']},
{id:'it038',cat:'interaction',sub:'Khalwah',marja:'makarem',q:'What is the ruling on khalwah (being alone with non-mahram)?',a:'Avoiding khalwah is both a religious guideline and a practical wisdom — it protects both parties from temptation and from suspicion. In modern workplaces, this translates to: open offices, glass-walled meeting rooms, and professional conduct. It\'s not about distrust but about creating safe boundaries that protect everyone involved.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['khalwah','seclusion','alone','non-mahram','private']},
,
{id:'ia039',cat:'interaction',sub:'Gender Interaction',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the rules for interaction between non-mahram men and women?',a:'Interaction between non-mahram men and women is permissible for legitimate purposes (business, education, medical treatment, social necessity) with conditions: (1) Hijab is observed, (2) No khalwah (being alone together in a closed space), (3) Conversation is purposeful and professional, (4) No physical contact except in necessity (medical treatment), (5) No flirtatious speech or behavior. Islam does not prohibit all interaction but regulates it.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['gender','interaction','rules','professional']},
{id:'ia040',cat:'interaction',sub:'Gender Interaction',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the rules for interaction between non-mahram men and women?',a:'Professional and educational interaction is permissible within Islamic boundaries. The key principles: maintain hijab, avoid unnecessary casual conversation, do not be alone together, keep interactions purposeful, and avoid situations that could lead to temptation. Workplace interaction, studying together, and community activities are all permissible with proper Islamic conduct.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['gender','workplace','education','boundaries']},
{id:'ia041',cat:'interaction',sub:'Neighbors',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the rights of neighbors in Islam?',a:'Islam places enormous emphasis on neighbors\' rights. The Prophet (s) said Jibrail continued to recommend kindness to neighbors until he thought neighbors would be given inheritance rights. Obligations include: not harming them, protecting their property, not spying on them, helping in times of need, sharing food, visiting when sick, attending their funerals, and not building structures that block their light or air without permission.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['neighbors','rights','kindness','prophet']},
{id:'ia042',cat:'interaction',sub:'Neighbors',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the rights of neighbors in Islam?',a:'Neighbors have profound rights in Islam — both Muslim and non-Muslim neighbors. The Prophet (s) said whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day should be good to their neighbor. This includes: greeting them, not causing noise or disturbance, sharing food, checking on their wellbeing, protecting their property in their absence, and tolerating minor inconveniences patiently.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['neighbors','muslim','nonmuslim','sharing']},
{id:'ia043',cat:'interaction',sub:'Parents',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the rights of parents in Islam?',a:'Parental rights are second only to Allah\'s rights. The Quran pairs worship of Allah with kindness to parents (17:23). Obligations include: obedience in permissible matters, financial support when needed, speaking gently (never saying \'uff\'), spending time with them, praying for them, fulfilling their wishes, and caring for them in old age. Disobedience to parents (uquq) is among the major sins.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['parents','rights','obedience','kindness']},
{id:'ia044',cat:'interaction',sub:'Parents',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the rights of parents in Islam?',a:'The Quran commands: \'Lower to them the wing of humility out of mercy\' (17:24). Parents have rights to: respectful treatment, financial support, physical care in old age, emotional connection, fulfillment of reasonable requests, and prayers after their death. The mother holds special status — the Prophet (s) mentioned her three times before the father when asked about who deserves the best companionship.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['parents','quran','mother','respect']},
{id:'ia045',cat:'interaction',sub:'Backbiting',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on backbiting (ghibah)?',a:'Backbiting — saying something true about a person behind their back that they would dislike — is haram. The Quran likens it to eating the flesh of your dead brother (49:12). It is one of the most common yet serious sins. Exceptions exist: warning someone about a dishonest business partner, seeking legal justice, identifying someone by a known characteristic, and responding to someone openly committing sin.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['backbiting','ghibah','haram','exceptions']},
{id:'ia046',cat:'interaction',sub:'Backbiting',marja:'makarem',q:'What is the ruling on backbiting (ghibah)?',a:'Ghibah is categorically forbidden. The Prophet (s) said it is worse than adultery because Allah may forgive the adulterer upon repentance but the backbiter needs the forgiveness of the person they harmed. To repent: seek forgiveness from the person if possible, or pray for them if approaching them would cause more harm. Listening to backbiting is also sinful — one should defend the absent person or leave.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['ghibah','forbidden','repentance','forgiveness']},
{id:'ia047',cat:'interaction',sub:'Lying',marja:'sistani',q:'Is lying ever permissible in Islam?',a:'Lying is generally haram and destroys trust. However, exceptions exist: (1) To save a life (hiding someone from an oppressor), (2) To reconcile between people (telling each that the other speaks well of them), (3) Between spouses to maintain harmony (compliments, reassurance), (4) In war strategy. These exceptions are narrow and specific — the general rule remains strict truthfulness. Tawriyah (equivocation) is preferred over direct lying when possible.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['lying','exceptions','tawriyah','truthfulness']},
{id:'ia048',cat:'interaction',sub:'Charity',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the etiquette of giving and receiving charity?',a:'Giving: should be done secretly when possible, without making the recipient feel indebted, without reminders, and with a pleasant attitude. The Quran says do not invalidate charity with reminders and hurt (2:264). Receiving: one should accept graciously if in genuine need, not take more than needed, and express gratitude to Allah and the giver. The best charity is that which the giver gives while fearing poverty themselves.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['charity','etiquette','secret','dignity']},
{id:'ia049',cat:'interaction',sub:'Animals',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the rights of animals in Islam?',a:'Islam mandates kindness to animals. Requirements: adequate food and water, shelter, not overburdening work animals, not striking animals on the face, using sharp instruments for halal slaughter to minimize suffering, not separating animal mothers from nursing young, and not using animals for entertainment fighting. The Prophet (s) said a woman entered Hell for starving a cat, and another was forgiven for giving water to a thirsty dog.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['animals','rights','kindness','prophet']},
{id:'ia050',cat:'interaction',sub:'Animals',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the rights of animals in Islam?',a:'Animals have recognized rights in Islam. The Prophet (s) established animal welfare long before modern movements: providing food and water, gentle treatment, not harming unnecessarily, and ensuring humane slaughter. Using animals for fighting is prohibited. Hunting for sport without eating the game is discouraged. Environmental stewardship — protecting habitats and biodiversity — extends from the principle of trusteeship of the earth.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['animals','welfare','environment','stewardship']},
{id:'ia051',cat:'interaction',sub:'Business Ethics',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the Islamic ethics of business relationships?',a:'Business relationships require: honesty in all dealings, fulfilling promises and contracts, timely payment of debts, fair pricing, not exploiting others\' ignorance or need, transparency about defects, avoiding conflicts of interest, and maintaining professional boundaries. The Prophet (s) said the honest merchant will be with the prophets and martyrs on the Day of Judgment.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['business','ethics','honesty','contracts']},
{id:'ia052',cat:'interaction',sub:'Hospitality',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the Islamic rules of hospitality?',a:'Hospitality is a deeply valued Islamic virtue. The host should: welcome guests warmly, serve food and drink, provide comfortable seating, not burden themselves beyond means, and accompany guests to the door. The guest should: not overstay, not be demanding, express gratitude, pray for the host, and bring a gift when visiting. The Prophet (s) said hospitality is three days — beyond that is charity.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['hospitality','guest','host','prophet']}
,
{id:'ia053',cat:'interaction',sub:'Racial Equality',marja:'sistani',q:'What is Islam\'s position on racial equality?',a:'Islam categorically rejects racism. The Quran states: We created you from male and female and made you peoples and tribes that you may know one another — the most noble among you is the most righteous (49:13). The Prophet (s) in his farewell sermon declared no Arab has superiority over a non-Arab except through piety. Racial discrimination in any form — social, economic, or legal — contradicts core Islamic teachings.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['racism','equality','quran','piety']},
{id:'ia054',cat:'interaction',sub:'Debt',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the Islamic ethics of lending and borrowing?',a:'Lending is highly rewarded — the Prophet (s) said lending to a Muslim in need is like giving charity. The lender should not charge interest, should be patient with a struggling borrower, and should not humiliate the debtor. The borrower must intend to repay, should not borrow beyond capacity, should repay promptly, and should express gratitude. The Quran recommends documenting loans in writing (2:282).',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['lending','borrowing','ethics','patience']}
,
{id:'ia039',cat:'interaction',sub:'Mahram',marja:'sistani',q:'Who are the mahram relatives in Islam?',a:'Mahram relatives (with whom marriage is permanently forbidden) include: parents, grandparents, children, grandchildren, siblings, uncles, aunts, nephews, nieces, parents-in-law, stepchildren (after consummation with their parent), foster relatives who share the nursing relationship. Between mahrams, physical contact (handshake, hug) and being without hijab are permissible. Non-mahram means any person of opposite gender who could theoretically be married.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['mahram','relatives','forbidden','marriage']},
{id:'ia040',cat:'interaction',sub:'Mahram',marja:'khamenei',q:'Who are the mahram relatives in Islam?',a:'Mahram categories: (1) Blood relations: parents, children, siblings, grandparents, grandchildren, uncles, aunts, (2) Marriage relations: parents-in-law, stepchildren, (3) Nursing relations: foster mother, foster siblings (with specific nursing conditions). The concept of mahram defines privacy boundaries — a woman can show her hair, shake hands, and be alone with mahram relatives. All others are non-mahram with whom Islamic gender etiquette applies.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['mahram','categories','blood','marriage']},
{id:'ia041',cat:'interaction',sub:'Handshake',marja:'sistani',q:'Is shaking hands with the opposite gender permissible?',a:'Physical contact including handshaking between non-mahram men and women is not permissible. In professional or social settings where refusing causes significant difficulty or offense, some scholars suggest using a barrier (gloves) or finding polite ways to decline. Cultural sensitivity is important — many non-Muslims do not understand this restriction. A respectful explanation or placing hand over heart as greeting is acceptable.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['handshake','prohibited','alternative','respect']},
{id:'ia042',cat:'interaction',sub:'Handshake',marja:'khamenei',q:'Is shaking hands with the opposite gender permissible?',a:'Touching non-mahram members of the opposite sex is not permissible. This includes handshaking. In situations where refusing causes genuine hardship (workplace, medical settings), some flexibility exists. Medical professionals treating patients of the opposite gender is permissible by necessity. The principle: minimize contact, maintain professional boundaries, and seek same-gender alternatives when possible.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['handshake','touch','prohibited','medical']},
{id:'ia043',cat:'interaction',sub:'Gender Interaction',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the rules for workplace interaction between genders?',a:'Professional workplace interaction is permissible with Islamic etiquette: maintain modest dress, avoid unnecessary private meetings (have a third person present), keep conversations professional, avoid flirtatious behavior, lower the gaze from inappropriate looking, and maintain professional boundaries. Islam does not prohibit women from working or interacting professionally — it establishes ethical boundaries to protect everyone\'s dignity.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['workplace','professional','boundaries','dignity']},
{id:'ia044',cat:'interaction',sub:'Gender Interaction',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the rules for workplace interaction between genders?',a:'Professional interaction is permissible within Islamic guidelines. Key principles: maintain hijab, avoid khalwah (being alone together), keep interactions purposeful and professional, do not engage in intimate or personal conversations, maintain respectful distance. Women\'s participation in the workforce, education, and public life is not prohibited — Islam provides guidelines to ensure these interactions remain dignified.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['workplace','khalwah','professional','participation']},
{id:'ia045',cat:'interaction',sub:'Neighbors',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the rights of neighbors in Islam?',a:'Neighbors have extensive rights: (1) Not being harmed by noise, smell, or encroachment, (2) Being greeted and treated kindly, (3) Receiving help in times of need, (4) Being visited when sick, (5) Having their privacy respected, (6) Being given food when you cook (recommended), (7) Not having their property damaged. The Prophet (s) said Jibreel kept emphasizing neighbors\' rights until he thought neighbors would be included in inheritance.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['neighbors','rights','kindness','prophet']},
{id:'ia046',cat:'interaction',sub:'Neighbors',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the rights of neighbors in Islam?',a:'Islam places great importance on neighborly relations. A person who sleeps full while their neighbor is hungry is not a true believer according to hadith. Neighbors\' rights include: safety from harm, kindness, help in difficulty, respect for privacy, and social courtesy. These rights apply regardless of the neighbor\'s religion. Good neighborly relations build strong communities and reflect Islamic values of compassion.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['neighbors','community','compassion','universal']},
{id:'ia047',cat:'interaction',sub:'Non-Muslims',marja:'sistani',q:'How should Muslims interact with non-Muslims?',a:'Islam commands kindness, justice, and good dealings with non-Muslims. The Quran states: \'Allah does not forbid you from being kind and just to those who do not fight you because of religion\' (60:8). Muslims should: be honest in business dealings, be good neighbors, show compassion, engage in respectful dialogue, and be ambassadors of Islamic values through their character. Forced conversion is explicitly forbidden.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['nonmuslims','kindness','justice','dialogue']},
{id:'ia048',cat:'interaction',sub:'Non-Muslims',marja:'khamenei',q:'How should Muslims interact with non-Muslims?',a:'The foundation is justice and kindness. Muslims should treat all people with dignity regardless of faith. Cooperation on matters of common good (charity, justice, environment) is encouraged. Respectful interfaith dialogue promotes understanding. What is prohibited: compromising Islamic beliefs to please others, participating in others\' religious rituals, and adopting practices that contradict Islam. The goal is coexistence with dignity.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['nonmuslims','coexistence','dignity','cooperation']},
{id:'ia049',cat:'interaction',sub:'Social Etiquette',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the Islamic etiquette for gatherings?',a:'Islamic gathering etiquette includes: giving salaam upon entering, sitting where there is space (not displacing others), participating positively in conversation, avoiding gossip and backbiting, not dominating the conversation, respecting elders, being kind to youth, keeping conversations beneficial, and saying salaam when leaving. Gatherings should ideally include dhikr (remembrance of Allah) and avoid purely idle talk.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['gatherings','etiquette','salaam','conversation']},
{id:'ia050',cat:'interaction',sub:'Social Etiquette',marja:'makarem',q:'What is the Islamic etiquette for gatherings?',a:'Islam provides comprehensive social etiquette: smile when meeting others, give salaam first, shake hands (same gender), make space for newcomers, speak gently, listen attentively, avoid interrupting, do not whisper while excluding others, avoid arguing, and leave with salaam. The Prophet (s) was the model of social grace — he gave full attention to whoever spoke with him and never made anyone feel insignificant.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['etiquette','prophet','grace','respect']},
{id:'ia051',cat:'interaction',sub:'Backbiting',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on backbiting (ghibah)?',a:'Backbiting — saying something about a person behind their back that they would dislike, even if true — is one of the major sins. The Quran compares it to eating the flesh of your dead brother (49:12). Exceptions exist: warning someone about a harmful person, testimony in court, seeking religious rulings, and correcting a public wrong. Repentance requires seeking forgiveness from the person if possible.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['backbiting','ghibah','major sin','exceptions']},
{id:'ia052',cat:'interaction',sub:'Backbiting',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the ruling on backbiting (ghibah)?',a:'Ghibah is severely condemned — it destroys social bonds and trust. Both the speaker and willing listener are sinful. In the digital age, this extends to online gossip, sharing embarrassing content, and negative comments about identifiable people. Limited exceptions apply: consulting about a business partner, warning about a dangerous person, or seeking justice. The default is silence about others\' faults.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['ghibah','digital','gossip','silence']}
,
{id:'ia053',cat:'interaction',sub:'Guests',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the rights of guests in Islam?',a:'Hosting guests is highly rewarded. Guest rights: (1) Be welcomed warmly, (2) Be offered the best food available, (3) Be given a clean, comfortable place, (4) Be treated with honor and respect, (5) Not be burdened with the host\'s problems. The host should: not ask if the guest wants food (just serve it), serve them first, accompany them to the door when leaving. Guest duration is three days — after which it becomes charity from the host.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['guests','hospitality','rights','honor']},
{id:'ia054',cat:'interaction',sub:'Parents',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the obligations toward parents?',a:'Parental rights are second only to Allah\'s rights. Obligations: (1) Obedience in permissible matters, (2) Kind speech always (never say \'uff\'), (3) Financial support when needed, (4) Regular visits and communication, (5) Making dua for them, (6) Fulfilling their reasonable wishes, (7) Patience with their aging and needs. These obligations remain even if parents are unjust or non-Muslim. Disobedience to parents (uquq al-walidayn) is a major sin.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['parents','obligations','kindness','major sin']},
{id:'ia055',cat:'interaction',sub:'Parents',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the obligations toward parents?',a:'The Quran repeatedly pairs worship of Allah with kindness to parents (17:23, 31:14). Obligations include: never raising one\'s voice, providing for their needs, visiting regularly, seeking their forgiveness, and making dua for them after their death. The mother has special status — the Prophet (s) said paradise is under the feet of mothers. Cutting ties with parents is among the greatest sins in Islam.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['parents','mother','quran','paradise']}
,
{id:'ia056',cat:'interaction',sub:'Business Ethics',marja:'sistani',q:'What are Islamic business ethics?',a:'Core principles: (1) Honesty in all transactions, (2) Fulfilling contracts and promises, (3) Fair pricing, (4) No deception or concealment of defects, (5) Prompt payment of debts and wages, (6) Not hoarding essential goods, (7) Not exploiting others\' ignorance or need, (8) Environmental responsibility, (9) Paying religious dues (khums, zakat) on profits. The Prophet (s) said the honest merchant will be with the prophets and martyrs on the Day of Judgment.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['business','ethics','honesty','prophet']},
{id:'ia057',cat:'interaction',sub:'Social Media Ethics',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the Islamic ethics for social media use?',a:'Social media ethics: (1) Verify information before sharing (Quran 49:6), (2) Do not engage in backbiting or gossip, (3) Maintain modesty in posts and photos, (4) Do not share others\' private information, (5) Avoid online arguments and trolling, (6) Use your platform for good, (7) Be respectful even in disagreement, (8) Protect your mental health from excessive comparison and negativity. Your online presence is a representation of your character and faith.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['social media','ethics','verification','modesty']}
,
{id:'ia200',cat:'interaction',sub:'Lying',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the detailed rulings on white lies and social courtesy?',a:'White lies for genuine benefit are discussed by scholars. Flattering someone to make them feel good (without deception that harms) is generally tolerated. Reconciliation lies (telling each party the other said something nice) are explicitly permitted. Courtesy statements (\'I\'m fine\' when not) are not considered lies by most scholars. However, habitual dishonesty even in small matters erodes character. The default must always be truthfulness.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['white lies','courtesy','reconciliation','truthful']},
{id:'ia201',cat:'interaction',sub:'Lying',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the detailed rulings on white lies and social courtesy?',a:'Islam has a nuanced view: lies that save lives, reconcile people, or protect honor (between spouses) have explicit permission. Social pleasantries (\'it was nice to see you\' when neutral) are not considered lies. However, the threshold for permissible \'white lies\' is higher than Western culture might assume. The believer\'s character should be known for truthfulness. Even where lying is permitted, using ambiguous truthful statements (tawriyah) is preferred.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['lying','tawriyah','permitted','character']}
,
{id:'ia300',cat:'interaction',sub:'Envy',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on envy (hasad)?',a:'Envy — wishing that another\'s blessings be removed — is a major sin. The Quran warns: \'Say I seek refuge from the evil of the envier when they envy\' (113:5). Envy eats good deeds like fire eats wood (hadith). However, ghibtah — wishing for similar blessings without wanting them removed from others — is permissible and even encouraged (competing in good deeds). The cure for envy includes: dua, gratitude, making dua for the envied person, and recognizing Allah\'s wisdom.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['envy','hasad','ghibtah','cure']},
{id:'ia301',cat:'interaction',sub:'Envy',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the ruling on envy (hasad)?',a:'Hasad is condemned because it implies dissatisfaction with Allah\'s distribution. The Quran and hadith consistently warn against it. It harms the envier spiritually before harming the envied. Remedies: strengthen faith in Allah\'s justice, practice gratitude, make dua for those you feel envious of (this neutralizes the feeling), recognize that everyone has unseen difficulties, and compete in good deeds rather than worldly possessions.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['envy','hasad','gratitude','faith']},
{id:'ia302',cat:'interaction',sub:'Suspicion',marja:'sistani',q:'What does Islam say about suspicion and mistrust?',a:'The Quran commands: \'Avoid much suspicion; indeed some suspicion is sin\' (49:12). One should assume the best of fellow believers unless there is clear evidence otherwise. Acting on suspicion — spying, investigating private matters, making accusations — is prohibited. If you witness something ambiguous about a Muslim, interpret it in the best possible way. However, reasonable caution in business dealings and entrusting responsibilities is prudent, not suspicion.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['suspicion','trust','best assumption','quran']},
{id:'ia303',cat:'interaction',sub:'Suspicion',marja:'khamenei',q:'What does Islam say about suspicion and mistrust?',a:'Islam builds society on trust, not suspicion. The believer should: assume positive intent, avoid spying and investigation of others\' private lives, not spread unverified information, and give others the benefit of the doubt. Suspicion poisons relationships and communities. The Quran specifically prohibits excessive suspicion alongside backbiting and spying (49:12). However, this does not mean naivety — verified evidence changes the situation from suspicion to knowledge.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['suspicion','trust','quran','evidence']},
{id:'ia304',cat:'interaction',sub:'Friendship',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the Islamic guidelines for choosing friends?',a:'Imam Ali (a) said: tell me who your friend is and I will tell you who you are. Guidelines: (1) Choose friends with strong faith and good character, (2) Avoid friends who lead you toward sin, (3) A true friend advises you honestly even when it\'s uncomfortable, (4) Friendship should be for the sake of Allah, (5) Maintain loyalty and support in difficulty, (6) Overlook minor faults but advise on serious matters. Cross-faith friendships based on mutual respect are permissible.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['friendship','choosing','faith','loyalty']},
{id:'ia305',cat:'interaction',sub:'Friendship',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the Islamic guidelines for choosing friends?',a:'Friends profoundly influence character and behavior. The Prophet (s) compared a good friend to a perfume seller (you benefit from their presence) and a bad friend to a blacksmith (you get burned). Choose friends who: remind you of Allah, support your growth, are honest with you, keep your secrets, and stand by you in difficulty. Avoid those who encourage sin, speak ill of others, or are unreliable. Quality of friendships matters more than quantity.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['friendship','influence','prophet','quality']}
,
{id:'ia300',cat:'interaction',sub:'Forgiveness',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the Islamic approach to forgiving others?',a:'Forgiveness is strongly encouraged: \'If you forgive, it is closer to taqwa\' (2:237). Benefits: (1) Divine reward — Allah forgives those who forgive others, (2) Inner peace — holding grudges harms the holder, (3) Social harmony, (4) Following the Prophetic example. However, forgiveness does not mean accepting continued abuse or waiving rights. One can forgive in their heart while still seeking justice. The highest form is forgiving those who have the least claim to forgiveness.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['forgiveness','taqwa','peace','justice']},
{id:'ia301',cat:'interaction',sub:'Forgiveness',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the Islamic approach to forgiving others?',a:'Islam teaches forgiveness as a virtue of the strong, not the weak. Forgiving when one has the power to retaliate is praised repeatedly in the Quran and hadith. The Prophet (s) forgave the people of Mecca who had persecuted him for years. Imam Ali (a) forgave his enemies when he held power. However, forgiveness is a personal choice — it cannot be imposed on victims. Forgiving personal wrongs while standing firm against societal injustice is the balanced Islamic approach.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['forgiveness','strength','prophet','balanced']}
];